<BaseLayout title="Types of Conveyors: 30+ Packaging Conveyor Systems Compared" description="30+ types of conveyors used in packaging. Roller, belt, chain, MDR, CDLR, accumulation, transfer, spiral and more, with specs and selection criteria." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Types of Conveyors for Packaging Operations</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Packaging operations use over 30 distinct conveyor types to transport, accumulate, sort, and elevate products between processes. The major categories include roller conveyors, belt conveyors, chain conveyors, accumulation conveyors, transfer conveyors, vertical conveyors, and sortation systems. Each type serves a specific function based on product weight, shape, speed requirements, and environment.</p>
<div class="photo-row"><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/curve-fmh-belt-curve-parcel.webp" width="1000" height="561" alt="A black curved belt conveyor in an aluminum frame turns product flow between straight roller conveyor sections in a distribution facility." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A curved belt conveyor joining straight conveyor runs that meet at an angle. The continuous belt carries product through the turn without the gaps of a roller curve. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://fmhconveyors.com/products/curved-belt-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FMH Conveyors</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/mdr-interroll-maersk-tote-lines.webp" width="1000" height="781" alt="Elevated view of roller conveyor lines with curves and an incline carrying gray totes past tall storage racking in a distribution center." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Tote conveyor lines at a Maersk distribution center in Melbourne, which Interroll reports uses 2.5 kilometers of its Modular Conveyor Platform. An MDR line is divided into short zones, each driven by its own motorized roller. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.interroll.com/references/details/maersk" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Interroll</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/spiral-ryson-mass-flow.webp" width="1000" height="1383" alt="Plastic bottles with green caps run back to back on a curved conveyor into a stainless steel spiral with a drive motor at its base." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Ryson stainless steel mass flow spiral carrying bottles back to back. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://ryson.com/spiral-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ryson</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>
<h2 id="all-types-heading">30+ Conveyor Types for Packaging</h2>
<p>Select a conveyor type below for detailed specifications, selection criteria, manufacturer references, and comparison data. Pages marked with a tag list indicate key sub-types covered.</p>
<ul class="usa-collection">{conveyorTypes.map((type) => (
<li class="usa-collection__item"><div class="usa-collection__body"><h3 class="usa-collection__heading"><a class="usa-link" href={type.slug}>{type.name}</a></h3><p class="usa-collection__description">{type.desc}</p>{type.tags && <p class="tags">{type.tags.map((tag: string) => <span class="usa-tag">{tag}</span>)}</p>}</div></li>
))}</ul>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Choose the Right Conveyor Type</h2>
<p>Five parameters drive every conveyor decision. Pin them down before you talk to a single vendor, or you'll get quoted on the wrong system.</p>
<h3>1. Product Characteristics</h3>
<p>Product weight, dimensions, shape, bottom surface condition, and fragility determine which conveyor types qualify. Flat-bottomed rigid cases suit <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>. Irregular shapes or soft-bottom bags require belt conveyors. Heavy pallets need <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> or pallet chain conveyors.</p>
<h3>2. Throughput Requirements</h3>
<p>Calculate your required conveyor speed using this formula: Speed (FPM) = products per minute x (product length + gap) in feet. Add headroom above the peak rate so the conveyor can catch up after a stop. Higher throughput applications may require <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller conveyors</a> or high-speed belt systems.</p>
<h3>3. Function Needed</h3>
<p>Transport is the baseline function. Beyond transport, you may need <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> (buffering between processes), <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer</a> (direction changes), sortation (routing to multiple destinations), or <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical transport</a> (elevation changes).</p>
<h3>4. Environment</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage operations</a> require <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary, washdown-rated conveyors</a> in stainless steel. Pharmaceutical plants need cleanroom-compatible systems sized to the room's <a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/53394.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ISO 14644-1</a> class. Standard warehouse environments can use painted carbon steel frames.</p>
<h3>5. Budget</h3>
<p>Conveyor cost varies by type, but length, controls, materials, and installation usually move the price more than the type label. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity roller conveyors</a> are the lowest cost option because they have no drive or controls. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> cost more upfront because every zone has a motor and controller, but zones run only when needed, which can lower energy and maintenance costs. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for detailed cost factor breakdowns.</p>
