<p>Source note: speed, load, and cost are relative classes for comparing types, not ratings. Rated speed and capacity depend on width, roller or chain pitch, drive size, and duty, so confirm them on the manufacturer's spec sheet for the model you quote. Cost depends mainly on length, controls, materials, and installation. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for cost factors.</p>
<h2 id="categories-heading">Conveyor Types by Category</h2>
<h3>Roller Conveyors</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">Roller conveyors</a> represent the largest conveyor category in packaging. They use cylindrical rollers mounted in side frames to transport flat-bottomed products. The four primary sub-types are <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> (unpowered), <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller</a> (belt-driven or line-shaft), <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> (motor driven roller), and <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> (chain driven live roller). Roller conveyors cover a wide load range, from light cartons on gravity sections to full pallets on CDLR.</p>
<h3>Belt Conveyors</h3>
<p>Belt conveyors use a continuous belt surface to transport products. They handle irregular shapes, soft-bottom bags, loose items, and incline/decline applications that roller conveyors cannot. Flat belt, cleated belt, and modular plastic belt are the three main sub-types. Dorner, mk North America, and Hytrol are major <a href="/conveyor-types/belt-conveyors/">belt conveyor manufacturers</a> for packaging applications.</p>
<h3>Accumulation Conveyors</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> buffer product between upstream and downstream processes. They absorb line stops, speed mismatches, and operator breaks without shutting down the entire <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a>. Zero-pressure accumulation (ZPA) and minimum-pressure accumulation are the two primary modes. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR technology</a> dominates new ZPA installations.</p>
<h3>Transfer &amp; Sortation Conveyors</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer conveyors</a> change product direction at right angles or multiple angles. Pop-up transfers, turntables, push diverters, and right-angle chain transfers handle different speed and load requirements. <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">Sortation systems</a> extend this concept to high-speed multi-lane routing for distribution and fulfillment applications.</p>
<h3>Vertical Conveyors</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">Vertical reciprocating conveyors (VRCs)</a> lift product between floor levels or mezzanines. They are a common alternative to freight elevators for moving goods, not people. Code treatment varies by jurisdiction, so confirm with the local inspector which standard applies before you buy. Spiral conveyors provide continuous vertical transport without the stop-and-go cycle of reciprocating units.</p>
<h3>Specialty Conveyors</h3>
<p>Specialty conveyor types address specific packaging needs. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/beverage/">Tabletop chain conveyors</a> handle bottles and cans on high-speed <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food and beverage lines</a>. Vibratory conveyors feed bulk parts and powders. <a href="/conveyor-types/magnetic-conveyors/">Magnetic conveyors</a> transport ferrous lids and cans. <a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">Vacuum conveyors</a> grip thin films, labels, and pouches that standard belts cannot hold.</p>
<h2 id="applications-heading">Conveyor Types by Industry</h2>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage packaging lines</a> typically use <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary stainless steel belt conveyors</a>, tabletop chain conveyors for bottles, and <a href="/conveyor-types/washdown-conveyors/">washdown-rated roller conveyors</a> for cased goods. <a href="https://www.ams.usda.gov/services/auditing/equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">USDA equipment review</a> and <a href="https://www.3-a.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">3-A sanitary standards</a> drive material and design requirements.</p>
<p><a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">General packaging lines</a> combine multiple conveyor types. A typical end-of-line system includes belt conveyors feeding a case packer, <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyors</a> transporting sealed cases, <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a> buffering before a palletizer, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyors</a> moving loaded pallets to stretch wrap and shipping.</p>
<p>Distribution and fulfillment centers rely on <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> for zone-controlled accumulation, sortation systems for multi-lane routing, and <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">VRCs</a> for mezzanine transport. <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">Automated conveyor systems</a> integrate these types with warehouse management software (WMS) for hands-free product flow.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">How many types of conveyors are there?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The packaging industry uses over 30 distinct conveyor types. Major categories include <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a> (gravity, powered, MDR, CDLR), belt conveyors (flat, cleated, modular), chain conveyors (tabletop, drag, slat), <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyors</a>, spiral conveyors, sortation systems, and <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical reciprocating conveyors</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the most common conveyor type in packaging?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt conveyors and <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a> are the two most common types. Belt conveyors handle lightweight and irregular products. Roller conveyors dominate case and carton transport. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR roller conveyors</a> have become the standard for zone-controlled accumulation in distribution centers.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How do I choose the right conveyor type?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Conveyor selection depends on five key factors: product characteristics (weight, size, shape, bottom surface), throughput requirement (units per minute), function needed (transport, accumulate, sort, elevate), environment (food-grade, washdown, cleanroom), and budget. Define these requirements first, then match them against conveyor type capabilities using the comparison table above.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What is the difference between a belt conveyor and a roller conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt conveyors use a continuous belt surface and carry products of any shape or bottom condition. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">Roller conveyors</a> use cylindrical rollers and require flat, rigid bottoms long enough to bridge several rollers at all times. Belt conveyors handle inclines and declines. Roller conveyors offer easier zone control, <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a>, and lower friction for heavy loads.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What conveyor types support zero-pressure accumulation?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Three conveyor types commonly provide zero-pressure accumulation (ZPA). <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> use independent low-voltage DC motorized zones. <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">Belt-driven live roller conveyors</a> use pneumatic clutches or band brakes per zone. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Dedicated accumulation conveyors</a> with photo-eye sensors and zone controllers manage product spacing. MDR dominates new ZPA installations.</p>

</div>
</div>
